Showed who is signed in on the toolbar button that opens the personal-tools menu, as their portrait or as their initials on a colour derived from their userid. Volto drew a generic user icon there, and inside the menu a 96px portrait — or, for the many users who have never uploaded one, a camera icon: the same picture for everybody, saying "no image here" rather than "this is you". The picture moves out of the menu and onto the button, where it is visible without opening anything.

The colour comes from the userid rather than the name, so somebody correcting the spelling of their own name does not change colour, and nothing is stored anywhere to keep it stable. Initials are the first letter of the first and last words — a middle name does not push the surname out — and a user with no name yet gets a plain circle rather than an empty box.

**This shadows two Volto components**, `Toolbar` and `PersonalTools`, because Volto has no extension point here: the only pluggable in `PersonalTools` is `toolbar-user-menu` at the end of the menu list, and `toolbar-personal` is a DOM id on a button rather than a pluggable. Both copies are verbatim apart from the avatar, and each carries a header naming the Volto version it was taken from and listing every line that differs, so a Volto upgrade is a diff against upstream rather than a re-reading. @ericof
